  {
   "name": "create_problem",
   "description": "Turn a question the student shares (pasted text, a screenshot, LeetCode/codehelp/anywhere) into a practice problem with LeetCode-style layout: description on the left, only class Solution in the editor, test cases checked automatically. The student approves it, then it opens on the Problem screen.\n\nHow tests run: the program is HARNESS + starter/solution + driver. The harness already includes <bits/stdc++.h>, \"using namespace std\", ListNode and TreeNode (LeetCode definitions), and:\n- judge::read<T>() reads one input line as T: int, long long, double, bool, char (\"a\"), string (\"abc\"), vector<...> of those (nested too, e.g. [[1,2],[3]]), ListNode* ([1,2,3]) and TreeNode* (level order with null).\n- judge::run(f) runs every case: f reads one case's input with judge::read and returns the answer, which is printed in LeetCode format and compared with \"expected\".\nSo a driver is usually just:\nint main() {\n    judge::run([] {\n        auto nums = judge::read<vector<int>>();\n        auto target = judge::read<int>();\n        return Solution().twoSum(nums, target);\n    });\n}\nFor in-place problems (void), call the method and return the modified argument. Don't define ListNode/TreeNode or main elsewhere.",
   "inputSchema": {
    "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
     "title": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "e.g. \"Two Sum\""
     },
     "difficulty": {
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
       "easy",
       "medium",
       "hard"
      ]
     },
     "description": {
      "type": "string",
      "description": "The problem statement in Markdown, with its examples (Input/Output/Explanation) and constraints, faithful to the source"
     },
     "params": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
       "type": "object",
       "properties": {
        "name": {
         "type": "string"
        },
        "type": {
         "type": "string",
         "description": "C++ type, e.g. vector<int>"
        }
       },
       "required": [
        "name",
        "type"
       ]
      },
      "description": "The function parameters, in order"
     },
     "compare": {
      "description": "any_order when the answer list can be in any order; any_order_nested when inner lists can also be (e.g. 3Sum triplets)",
      "type": "string",
      "enum": [
       "exact",
       "any_order",
       "any_order_nested"
      ]
     },
     "tests": {
      "type": "array",
      "items": {
